True.  But a polished silver shield works the same way,
as does an amulet of reflection.  The in-game wording
says that the (breath, wand, spell, or whatever) ray
"reflects off your" amulet or armor or shield, but it
simply ignores the question of what would happen to
your inventory.

I view this aspect of reflection (which makes it quite
a bit more useful and powerful than it would otherwise
be) as a compromise, trading off some realism for more
game playability.

It would be interesting to play a variant where both
reflection and magic resistance are somewhat less
powerful than in vanilla, in a way that keeps them 
balanced and still about equally desirable with one 
another.  IMO, MR should not protect against teleport
(because that's what teleport control is for) or death
rays or finger of death (because death resistance 
should be a distinct thing, just as disintegration 
resistance is) or polymorph (because that's what
polymorph control is for) or drain strength (IMO,
drain resistance should handle that).  Additionally,
it is tempting to say that striking or force bolt
or magic missile should be resisted by a thing
called "force resistance", except that leaves
magic resistance protecting against very little,
so perhaps *one* of these categories should be 
left merged with MR.  Given all that, reflection
as it currently stands would clearly be MUCH
more important than any one of the resistances
currently lumped together under MR, so to keep
things balanced, reflection would need to have
somewhat reduced utility.  Not protecting your
inventory is an obvious step in that direction.
It would be tempting, in addition, to make some
of the existing ray attacks into beam attacks,
probably starting with sleep.

